# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Opening statements: a direct answer to each indicator's question, at most 1,000 characters.
Every fact comes from Compendium v4. Where the record has a gap the statement says so."""
INTRO = {
'15.2.1': "Yes. In 2025 MMSU supported and organised events that bring land stewardship to students, farmers and communities. About 230 CAFSD graduating students planted 120 native guijo and yakal seedlings and 130 bamboo propagules in the University Forest Reserves on 2 July. For World Environment Day, 250 mangrove propagules went into the Currimao shoreline, and on 24 July 1,000 coconut seedlings from the Philippine Coconut Authority were planted along the Currimao beachfront under the Green Wall Program. The President’s Farm Field Day on 28 October showed eight rice varieties, including options for saline and rainfed land, and the 7th Parambak ditoy MMSU on 14 February opened extension technologies to 37 exhibitors and their partners. A regenerative-farming talk at the National Science and Technology Week forum and a wildlife photo contest for the International Day for Biological Diversity widened the reach. Graduation tree planting recurs yearly, from 2024 to 2026.",
'15.2.2': "MMSU has not yet recorded a dated policy that food on campus is sustainably farmed; a sourcing memorandum from the Business Directorate would supply it. What exists is practice on university land. In May 2025, 18 researchers and farm workers trained to run a new Php 2.2-million greenhouse in the research, development and extension complex, built under HERIP with three bays for soilless, hydroponic and soil-based crops. At the 7th Parambak ditoy MMSU on 14 February 2025, participants harvested fresh vegetables at the President’s Farm while 37 exhibitors showed technologies such as off-season tomato and shallot production and indigenous vegetables, and the incoming President urged the units concerned to sustain sustainable farming practices. These activities show how food is grown and demonstrated on campus, and they are the base on which a written policy can stand.",
'15.2.3': "Yes. MMSU manages 1,068.13 hectares of forest reserves in Lubbot and Payao, Batac, and extends them with stock from its own Central and Bamboo Nurseries: on 2 July 2025, 120 native guijo and yakal seedlings and 130 bamboo propagules were planted. Its Indigenous Food Plants Program has documented 46 wild food plants in seven towns and conserves them in a botanical garden, an alukon genebank, wild-yam cultivation and community foodscapes; a second batch of foodscapes and seedbanks was set up in October 2025, and the Regional Development Council of Region 1 adopted the programme. Under a DOST-PCAARRD nature-based solutions project, bamboo-engineered structures help young mangroves survive in Badoc and Currimao, and on 6 December MMSU, the Rotary Club of Laoag Sunshine and LGU Badoc planted 280 mangrove propagules in a typhoon-damaged stand. A new bambusetum was also established in Pinili.",
'15.2.4': "Yes. In 2025 MMSU taught communities about the ecosystems around them. On 27–28 October, a two-day seminar-workshop trained representatives of Currimao, Pasuquin, Badoc and Burgos, with DENR-PENRO officials, in mangrove ecology, nature-based solutions, legal protection and the nursery propagation of Avicennia and Sonneratia, while the Pedada People’s Organization shared its community experience. On 2 May, 64 farmers in Barangay 11 Sta. Rosa, Sarrat, learned to propagate alukon, an indigenous vegetable tree, at the community’s request. On 3–4 December, the Information and Technology Caravan brought lectures on one-node bamboo propagation and an alukon demonstration to the Siwawer Farmers’ Festival in Vintar. In October, community foodscapes and seedbanks in San Nicolas became learning gardens for kapaskapas, alokon, pako, dalayap, wild singkamas and roselle, and the botanical garden keeps sharing planting materials with schools and communities.",
'15.2.5': "Yes. MMSU takes sustainable farming to communities through several channels. Its School-on-the-Air opened a season on regenerative vegetable farming on 24–25 June 2025 with 1,000 enrollees from all 22 municipalities and cities of Ilocos Norte, broadcast over three radio stations until December. The President’s Farm Field Day on 28 October showed farmers eight rice varieties, two of them for saline and rainfed fields. In Pinili, 46 farmers and community members learned bamboo propagation and plantation management. In Pasuquin, a vermicast facility helps onion farmers restore degraded soil, and a 12 November training on good agricultural practices doubled participation from 15 to 30 farmers. On 19 September, 59 Philippine Science High School students observed sustainable farming at the Technology Demonstration Farm. MMSU also leads a soil health research programme for rice-based cropping systems with Griffith University.",
'15.2.6': "Farm tourism is taking shape at MMSU, though a structured community training on it is not yet recorded. In 2025 the university and DA-PhilRice transplanted the eighth annual rice paddy art at the Extension Demonstration Area in Batac (7 July) and unveiled it on 22 August, with about 50 BS Agriculture students as guides; the Dingras Campus unveiled a second paddy art on 9 September, in line with the Farm Tourism Development Act. Continuing work points the same way. In January 2026 DOST-PCAARRD and UP-ISSI assessed TALON, a farm tourism model that joins agriculture, tourism and education and has the approval of the PCAARRD Governing Council. The Biodiversity Conservation Learning Center and Leisure Park, planted with 28 native tree species, serves as a field laboratory and a planned health and wellness tourism hub, and an MMSU expert helps prepare the communication plan that promotes the Kalbario-Patapat Natural Park as an ecotourism destination.",
'15.3.1': "Yes. MMSU’s Land Use, Zoning, Water and Energy Policies, adopted in 2021 as part of Sirmata 2040, its Land Use Development and Infrastructure Plan prepared under Republic Act 11396, govern the conservation, restoration and sustainable use of all university lands. Policy 1.3 requires land use to maintain, protect, conserve, restore and enhance the integrity of the land, the environment and ecological balance on every campus. Policy 7.1 requires valid and reliable science before any land is altered or converted, and Policy 10.2 gives fragile lands and protected areas special management. Under Policy 9.1, protected landholdings are conserved and excluded from any alteration except as permitted by law, and the MMSU Forest Reserve is maintained as a no-build zone. Policy 4.3 commits the university to review, update and promptly communicate these policies regularly.",
'15.3.2': "MMSU has long identified and conserved threatened plants, although a species policy or register is not yet recorded. In 2003, researchers surveyed Laoag City, Batac, Pasuquin, San Nicolas and Dingras: of 124 plants that elders named as vanishing, 77 were validated, 45 as endangered and 32 as vulnerable, using IUCN criteria applied locally (this is not the IUCN Red List). A 2011 study published by SEARCA documented 46 indigenous food plants from 27 families in seven upland and remote municipalities. These findings grew into the Indigenous Food Plants Program, which protects the plants through a botanical garden, an alukon genebank at the MMSU Forest Reserve, circa situ cultivation of wild yams and community foodscapes. In 2025 it trained 64 farmers in alukon propagation and extended seedbanks to Vintar, and the Regional Development Council of Region 1 adopted it as its first research and development initiative.",
'15.3.3': "Yes. Sirmata 2040, MMSU’s Land Use Development and Infrastructure Plan, is the university’s 20-year guide for its land and infrastructure. Prepared in 2021 under Republic Act 11396 with a Php 2 million grant from the Commission on Higher Education, it covers the campuses and the reserve areas in Nagbacsan, Payao, Lubbot and Sarnap, including the forest reserves where forestry and agroforestry research is set up. Local biodiversity is built into its development rules: Protected Land is conserved and excluded from any alteration except as permitted by law (Policy 9.1), the MMSU Forest Reserve is kept as a no-build zone, and before any land is altered or converted the plan requires a science-based assessment of its natural suitability and carrying capacity (Policies 7.1, 10.1 and 11.1). Stakeholder consultations, sectoral studies and campus planning workshops shaped the plan, and the CHED Technical Working Group endorsed it after validation visits.",
'15.3.4': "No policy on alien species is recorded yet. Nothing was found in MMSU’s public sources, the focal person’s mailbox or the links supplied by the team. A memorandum from the Office of the President requiring native species in campus planting and the control of invasive species would answer the question. The practice it would formalise is already visible. CAFSD graduating classes plant native guijo, yakal, banaba and bamboo in the forest reserves; the Pasuquin mangrove planting uses the native lapis-lapis and arinaya; and 28 native tree species and two bamboo species have been planted at the Biodiversity Conservation Learning Center and Leisure Park. Community foodscapes grow indigenous food plants. What is missing is the written policy, with a responsible office, that states the rule and how invasive species will be controlled.",
'15.3.5': "Yes. In 2025 MMSU worked with communities, local governments and agencies on land and coast they share. On 29 November it signed a memorandum of agreement with the Toyota Motor Philippines Foundation, DENR and LGU Pasuquin to conserve a degraded two-hectare coastal forest in Barangay Davila, where about 110 volunteers planted 1,750 mangrove seedlings and 3,500 more are planned over two years. In June, MMSU planted 250 mangrove propagules in Currimao with the LGU, DENR and fisherfolk organisations, and on 6 December it planted 280 more in Badoc with the Rotary Club of Laoag Sunshine and the LGU. A dialogue with DENR Region 1 brought pledges of technical support for restoration and monitoring. In Vintar and San Nicolas, schools and barangays maintain indigenous food plant foodscapes, and in Pinili a community bambusetum was established. An MMSU expert also sits on the Kalbario-Patapat Natural Park technical working group.",
'15.4.1': "Yes. MMSU’s Water Policy, part of Sirmata 2040 (2021) and based on the Water Code of the Philippines, requires that all water and related resources of the university, ground and surface, be shielded from any kind of pollution (Policy 2.9) and that polluted resources be rehabilitated immediately, with attention to impoundments, catch basins and water treatment facilities (Policy 2.8). The Integrated Pollution and Waste Management Program, revised in 2020, provides for registration as a hazardous waste generator for wastewater from agro-industrial facilities such as the piggery, in compliance with the Clean Water Act (RA 9275). Memorandum No. 26-370 of 30 September 2026 adds a compliance programme covering wastewater, with DENR-EMB permits, monitoring and reporting. From February 2024 to June 2025, 17 wells on the MMSU Farm were also checked monthly for pH, dissolved solids, conductivity, chloride and nitrate-nitrogen.",
'15.4.2': "Yes. MMSU’s Integrated Waste Management Program, approved by the Board of Regents in 2009, amended in 2015 and revised as the Integrated Pollution and Waste Management Program, prohibits Styrofoam for serving or packing food in the Food Service Unit and limits plastics; customers dining out bring their own containers. A policy of 11 February 2016 eliminates Styrofoam and plastic as secondary packaging in favour of paper bags, cartons, eco-bags and reusable containers. The Office of the President’s “No Bottled Water” policy, issued in Memoranda No. 22-148A (2022) and No. 23-189 (2023), ends bottled water at Administrative Council meetings, and CAFSD banned plastic bottled water in its auxiliary buildings in October 2024. On 1 July 2025 MMSU signed a commitment with DENR-EMB Region I to support solid waste management under RA 9003 and RA 11898, and on 24 October student advocates held a monthly campus clean-up that collected about 30 bags of waste.",
'15.4.3': "Yes. The Integrated Pollution and Waste Management Program, revised in 2020 and implemented by the Pollution Control Unit and the General Services directorate, sets out how hazardous materials are handled. It assigns focal persons for hazardous wastes, air and water pollution, solid wastes, and PCBs; unit waste coordinators must store and label hazardous wastes before collection; and units that generate hazardous waste are registered as generators. Wastes are kept in centralised, separate storage, including for PCBs, for up to one year under Republic Act 6969 and its storage guidelines, then disposed of through a DENR-accredited transport, storage and disposal facility in coordination with DENR-EMB. Memorandum No. 26-370 of 30 September 2026 adds an inventory of PCB-containing equipment, written clearance from the Sustainability and Futures Thinking Office before any such equipment is moved or disposed of, and records of manifests and certificates of treatment.",
}

if __name__ == '__main__':
    for k, v in INTRO.items(): print(k, len(v), 'OK' if len(v) <= 1000 else 'OVER')
